    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d43">

                <pb facs="0128" n="48" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg48"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/739a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chairman of the
                        American Delegation</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-03-05T20:00:00-05:00">March 5, 1930—8
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>175. For Senator Reed from Huntley.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d43-f1"/> Senator Moses
                    told me today he does not share the view that a consultative treaty cannot be
                    put through the Senate, and says that he does not see why anyone who subscribed
                    to the Knox formula<anchor n="68" xml:id="d43-f2"/> in the League of Nations
                    fight cannot support such a treaty if one should be signed as a by-product of
                    the London Conference. Considering Moses’ prominence in anti-League fight, this
                    statement is very significant. Senator Watson says Fess sounded Senate key-note
                    on Conference yesterday when, in the course of speech, he summarized
                    administration achievements and reviewed Conference background.<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d43-f3"/></p>
                <p>The principal features relating to this Conference of this speech are:</p>
                <p>[Here follows a summary of the speech.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d43-f1" n="67" xml:id="d43-f1-content">
                <p>T. A. Huntley, secretary to Senator Reed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d43-f2" n="68" xml:id="d43-f2-content">
                <p>The form of reservation proposed by Senator Knox of Pennsylvania on November 6,
                    1919, for consent to the ratification of the Treaty of Versailles (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Treaties, Conventions, etc., Between the United States
                        of America and Other-Powers</hi>, 1910–1923 (Washington, Government Printing
                    Office, 1923), vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, p. 3329) in such terms
                    as would make the United States a consulting member of the League of Nations;
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Congressional Record</hi>, vol. 58, pt. 8, p. 8000,
                    pt. 9, p. 8742.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d43-f3" n="69" xml:id="d43-f3-content">
                <p>Speech delivered by Simeon D. Fess, Senator from Ohio, on March 4, 1930; <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Congressional Record</hi>, vol. 72, pt. 5, p. 4666.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
